Build an explicit output object instead of spreading the model.

```js
const preview = post => ({ id: post.id, title: post.title });
const post = { id: 7, title: 'Release notes', body: 'Details', ownerEmail: 'private@example.test' };
console.log(preview(post)); // { id: 7, title: 'Release notes' }
```

The output contract is independent of the storage model. Adding a private model field later must not automatically add it to the response. This example needs no undefined Author, Post, or serializer classes.

**Check:** Assert the exact output keys and verify the input object is unchanged. An explicit response shape complements authorization; it does not replace it.

```js
function publicPreview(post) {
  return { id: post.id, title: post.title };
}
```

This is an output allowlist. `{ ...post }` would leak body and ownerEmail; deleting fields from post would mutate the caller's model.

**Check:** Call the function with an additional private field and assert that the result still has exactly two keys. Confirm the original object retains all of its fields.

Use details with a summary label.

```jsx
function Explanation() {
  return (
    <details>
      <summary>Why did this save fail?</summary>
      <p>The title was empty. Enter a title and try again.</p>
    </details>
  );
}
```

The browser owns the disclosure interaction and open state. Keep the summary meaningful and verify the native control in the browsers and assistive technology relevant to the product.

**Check:** Reach the summary with Tab and toggle it with the keyboard. The explanation should appear and disappear without submitting a surrounding form.

```jsx
function SaveDetails() {
  return (
    <details>
      <summary>Save details</summary>
      <p>Your draft is saved locally.</p>
    </details>
  );
}
```

summary supplies the interactive label and details contains the revealed content. A clickable div would require recreating keyboard and state semantics.

**Check:** Toggle by mouse and keyboard. Confirm the hidden paragraph becomes visible and that the label remains available while collapsed.

The expanded value is inverted, and the state belongs on the controlling button. Use aria-expanded={!isCollapsed} on that button and connect it to the panel with aria-controls.

```jsx
function Section({ open, onToggle, buttonId, panelId, label, children }) {
  return (
    <section>
      <h3><button type="button" id={buttonId}
        aria-expanded={open} aria-controls={panelId}
        onClick={onToggle}>{label}</button></h3>
      <div id={panelId} hidden={!open}>{children}</div>
    </section>
  );
}
```

The parent supplies unique IDs and toggles open. This example is one accordion section; multi-section policy belongs to its parent.

**Check:** When open is false, the panel is hidden and the button reports false. Toggle once and verify both switch together.

```js
function nextIndex(index, length) {
  return length === 0 ? null : (index + 1) % length;
}
```

The empty case is explicit instead of producing NaN. null means there is no selected item, so the caller must render an empty state rather than index into the array.

**Check:** nextIndex(0, 0) is null, nextIndex(0, 1) is 0, nextIndex(1, 3) is 2, and nextIndex(2, 3) is 0.

```jsx
function onKeyDown(event) {
  if (event.key !== 'Enter' || event.nativeEvent.isComposing) return;
  event.preventDefault();
  const tag = event.currentTarget.value.trim();
  if (!tag) return;
  setTags(current => current.includes(tag) ? current : [...current, tag]);
  setDraft('');
}
```

This handler assumes a controlled input with setDraft and a tag array with setTags. Duplicate matching is case-sensitive here; choose normalization deliberately. Use labeled buttons to remove individual tags.

**Check:** Try blank input, an existing tag, a new tag, and Enter during composition. Only the new completed tag should be appended, and Enter should not submit the enclosing form.
